  "summary": "Spatial and pathway-level descriptors now allow condition-specific regulatory organization to be inferred and compared directly across healthy, diseased, and tumor tissue.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is inferring how regulatory and pathway organization differs between conditions, using descriptors that retain tissue or pathway structure rather than bulk gene lists. Two liver preprints apply spatial transcriptomics to Visium data. One uses causal discovery to separate local from neighbor-derived effects on senescence. The other uses spatial correlation functions to extract per-gene susceptibility and correlation length, with zonation loss in fibrotic liver as the readout. The cancer preprint applies a related logic at the pathway level, scoring Reactome deregulation, organizing samples by minimum spanning trees, and comparing normal and tumor states. The new element is treating spatial context and pathway-level deregulation as explicit variables in condition-specific comparisons. All three items are preprints, and the summaries provided do not report independent validation, so conclusions remain provisional.",
